Let the square have side $s$. Since $F$ and $G$ lie on $BC$, the side $DE$ is parallel to $BC$ at distance $s$ from it. Triangle $ADE$ is then similar to triangle $ABC$ (with $D\in AB$, $E\in AC$), and the ratio of similarity is the ratio of the distances from $A$ to the lines $DE$ and $BC$. So
\[
\dfrac{DE}{BC}=\dfrac{|2-s|}{2}\quad\text{if $DE$ is on the same side of $BC$ as $A$,}\qquad
\dfrac{DE}{BC}=\dfrac{2+s}{2}\quad\text{otherwise.}
\]
With $DE=s$ and $BC=6$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $DE$ between $BC$ and $A$ ($s<2$): $s=3(2-s)$, so $s=\dfrac{3}{2}$; this is the square inscribed in the triangle, figure (i).
\item $DE$ beyond $A$ ($s>2$): $s=3(s-2)$, so $s=3$; here $D$ and $E$ lie on the extensions of $AB$ and $AC$ beyond $A$, figure (ii).
\item $DE$ on the other side of $BC$: $s=3(2+s)$ has no positive solution.
\end{itemize}

