So the perimeter of $DEFG$ is $4\cdot\dfrac{3}{2}=\boxed{6}$ for the square inside the triangle, and $4\cdot3=\boxed{12}$ for the square on the extensions beyond $A$. (The computation does not depend on the shape of the triangle; in figure (ii) the square overlaps the triangle, and $A$ lies inside it.)

The side from $(-4,0)$ to $(0,5)$ has slope $\dfrac{5}{4}$, so the line $y=\dfrac{5}{4}x+c$ is parallel to it. For $c>0$ it cuts off a small triangle at the vertex $(6,0)$, similar to the whole triangle, and the rest is a trapezoid.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(6.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair A=(-4,0),B=(6,0),C=(0,5),P=(-2/3,0),Q=(2,10/3);
fill(P--B--Q--cycle,palecyan);
draw((-5,0)--(7,0),gray,Arrow);draw((0,0)--(0,6),gray,Arrow);
draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(P--Q,blue+linewidth(0.9));
dot(A);dot(B);dot(C);dot(P);dot(Q);
label("$(-4,0)$",A,S);label("$(6,0)$",B,S);label("$(0,5)$",C,NE);
label("$-\dfrac{2}{3}$",P,S);label("$Q$",Q,NE);label("$x$",(7,0),E);label("$y$",(0,6),N);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

The small triangle has $\dfrac{4}{5+4}=\dfrac{4}{9}$ of the area of the whole triangle, so its linear ratio of similarity is $\sqrt{\dfrac{4}{9}}=\dfrac{2}{3}$. Its side on the $x$-axis is $\dfrac{2}{3}\cdot10=\dfrac{20}{3}$, so the line meets the $x$-axis at $x=6-\dfrac{20}{3}=-\dfrac{2}{3}$. Then $0=\dfrac{5}{4}\cdot\left(-\dfrac{2}{3}\right)+c$, so
\[
c=\boxed{\dfrac{5}{6}}.
\]
(Check: the line meets the side $y=5-\dfrac{5}{6}x$ at $Q=\left(2,\dfrac{10}{3}\right)$; the small triangle has area $\dfrac{1}{2}\cdot\dfrac{20}{3}\cdot\dfrac{10}{3}=\dfrac{100}{9}$ and the whole triangle $25$, and $25-\dfrac{100}{9}=\dfrac{125}{9}$, ratio $125:100=5:4$.)

Put $X$ at the origin with the lines $AB$ and $DC$ horizontal: $AB$ is the line $y=5$ and $DC$ the line $y=-7$ (distances in the ratio $AX:XC=5:7$). The dilation about $X$ with factor $-\dfrac{7}{5}$ maps $A$ to $C$ and $B$ to $D$, so write $A=(a,5)$, $B=(b,5)$, $C=\left(-\dfrac{7a}{5},-7\right)$, $D=\left(-\dfrac{7b}{5},-7\right)$.

The points of $PQ$ that divide it into three equal parts lie at heights $5-\dfrac{12}{3}=1$ and $5-\dfrac{24}{3}=-3$. The point of $BD$ at height $1$ is $M=\left(\dfrac {b}{5},1\right)$ and the point of $AC$ at height $-3$ is $N=\left(-\dfrac{3a}{5},-3\right)$. Let $P=(p,5)$ and let $d$ be the horizontal step from $P$ to $M$ (equal to the step from $M$ to $N$):
\[
p+d=\dfrac {b}{5},\qquad p+2d=-\dfrac{3a}{5}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad d=-\dfrac{3a+b}{5},\quad p=\dfrac{3a+2b}{5}.
\]

\begin{asy}
